1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,941
Every app starts with one database.

2
00:00:03,091 --> 00:00:05,058
Then people actually show up.

3
00:00:05,757 --> 00:00:08,361
Traffic doubles. Then it doubles again.

4
00:00:08,512 --> 00:00:14,363
Your p99 latency — the slowest one percent of
requests — starts to climb.

5
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:16,555
And that's the part your users feel.

6
00:00:17,255 --> 00:00:19,601
The first fix is a bigger machine.

7
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:22,930
It works — until there's no bigger machine to buy.

8
00:00:23,631 --> 00:00:25,131
So you split the data.

9
00:00:25,281 --> 00:00:30,339
Sharding cuts one huge table into smaller pieces,
each on its own server.

10
00:00:30,489 --> 00:00:37,477
A routing layer reads a key, like the user ID, and
sends each query to the one shard that owns it.

11
00:00:38,176 --> 00:00:41,465
Now every server carries only a slice of the load.

12
00:00:41,615 --> 00:00:44,051
Need more room? Add another shard.

13
00:00:44,201 --> 00:00:47,843
Capacity grows sideways, instead of hitting a
ceiling.

14
00:00:48,543 --> 00:00:54,112
The hard part is doing it live: moving data between
shards while traffic keeps flowing.

15
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:57,802
That's exactly what systems like Vitess were built
for.

16
00:00:58,502 --> 00:01:00,800
So one database becomes many.

17
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,772
Your app never notices.
